  annotation = {中文辅助译名：通过双分支特征提取和级联条件扩散进行高质量脑电图到图像重建},
  abstract = {Reconstructing visual stimuli from electroen-cephalography (EEG) signals holds significant value for uncovering the underlying mechanisms of the human visual system. However, due to the inherently low signal-to-noise ratio and limited spatiotemporal resolution of EEG, current reconstruction methods face substantial challenges in achieving precise semantic alignment and high-fidelity image generation. Moreover, some prior EEG reconstruction approaches rely on additional large-scale EEG data for representation pretraining. To address these challenges, we propose MindScryer, a novel end-to-end framework for EEG-based image reconstruction. The proposed framework employs a dual-branch encoder. One branch incorporates a self-supervised Transformer to extract temporal features, while the other utilizes a depthwise separable convolutional module to capture frequency-spatial representations. This architecture enhances the EEG signal representation across multiple dimensions. Next, an improved contrastive learning mechanism is introduced to align the EEG embeddings with CLIP embeddings derived from textual descriptions generated by a large language model. This approach highlights key visual semantics and mitigates cross-modal heterogeneity. Finally, a cascaded diffusion model integrates both fine-grained aligned EEG embeddings and coarse-grained categorical information to synthesize high-quality images with improved semantic consistency. Experimental results on the BDVE dataset demonst},
}

